# Converted to the revised ten-section template (plus section 11); content and tables carried from the previous version.
BOOK = 'Ruth'
TESTAMENT = 'Old Testament'
GENRE = 'history'
SUBTITLE = 'A Moabitess in Israel, the Kinsman’s Redemption, and the Line to David'
SECTIONS = [(1, 'Book Setting & Program Identity', [('p', 'This section introduces you to the book by explaining who wrote it, who received it, and what was happening when it was written. It also shows you which of God’s programs the book belongs to — Israel’s prophetic program or the Body of Christ’s mystery program. Knowing the setting and the program identity helps you read the book in its proper context and understand how its teachings fit into the larger story of Scripture.'), ('p', 'Ruth is a short account set in the days of the judges. It follows Naomi and her daughter in law Ruth the Moabitess, Ruth’s vow to Naomi, her gleaning in the field of Boaz, his place as a near kinsman, his purchase of the inheritance, and the birth of Obed, who is “the father of Jesse, the father of David” (Ruth 1:16; 2:2; 3:9; 4:10, 17).'), ('box', 'Program Identity:', 'This book belongs entirely to the Prophecy Program, where Israel’s land, inheritance and kinsman law shape every scene and the line runs to David (Ruth 4:10, 17, 22).')]),
(2, 'Narrative Flow of Ruth', [('p', 'This section gives you a simple walkthrough of how the book unfolds. Instead of diving into details right away, it shows the major movements in the book — how its message begins, how it develops, and how it builds toward its close. Seeing the flow of the book helps you understand the big picture before you study the individual passages.'), ('h3', 'Movement 1 — Naomi and Ruth (Ch. 1)'), ('p', 'Ruth tells Naomi, “thy people shall be my people, and thy God my God,” and says she will be buried where Naomi dies (Ruth 1:16–17).'), ('h3', 'Movement 2 — Gleaning in Boaz’s Field (Ch. 2)'), ('p', 'Ruth goes to glean, Boaz speaks a blessing over her, and Naomi names him as one of their next kinsmen (Ruth 2:2, 12, 20).'), ('h3', 'Movement 3 — At the Threshing Floor (Ch. 3)'), ('p', 'Ruth asks Boaz to spread his skirt over her as a near kinsman, and he answers that the city knows she is a virtuous woman (Ruth 3:9, 11).'), ('h3', 'Movement 4 — The Redemption and the Line to David (Ch. 4)'), ('p', 'Boaz purchases the inheritance, takes Ruth as his wife, and Obed is born, the father of Jesse, the father of David (Ruth 4:10, 13–14, 17, 22).')]),
(3, 'Program Themes in Ruth', [('p', 'This section highlights the major ideas emphasized in the book — the big doctrinal themes that shape how Israel should think and live under the covenants. These themes are not verse-by-verse explanations; they are the book’s main emphases, the repeated ideas God wants Israel to carry forward. Seeing these themes together helps you understand the book’s message before you look at the individual truths in the tables.'), ('ol', ['<b>Loyalty to Naomi and Her God</b> — “Whither thou goest, I will go; and where thou lodgest, I will lodge: thy people shall be my people, and thy God my God” (Ruth 1:16).', '<b>The LORD Recompenses</b> — “The LORD recompense thy work, and a full reward be given thee of the LORD God of Israel, under whose wings thou art come to trust” (Ruth 2:12).', '<b>The Near Kinsman</b> — “I am Ruth thine handmaid: spread therefore thy skirt over thine handmaid; for thou art a near kinsman” (Ruth 3:9).', '<b>The Inheritance Purchased</b> — “To raise up the name of the dead upon his inheritance, that the name of the dead be not cut off from among his brethren” (Ruth 4:10).', '<b>The Line to David</b> — “They called his name Obed: he is the father of Jesse, the father of David” (Ruth 4:17).'])]),
(4, 'Christ in Ruth', [('p', 'This section gathers what the book teaches about Christ — through foreshadowing, typology, and prophetic anticipation. These statements help you see the book’s Christ-centered trajectory before you study the doctrinal truths in detail. By collecting these points together, you get a clear picture of how Ruth points to Christ.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Kinsman</b> — Boaz acts as a near kinsman who purchases the inheritance (Ruth 3:9; 4:10).', '<b>The Line to David</b> — The book closes with “Obed begat Jesse, and Jesse begat David” (Ruth 4:22).', '<b>The Gentile in Israel’s Line</b> — A Moabitess comes under the wings of the LORD God of Israel and stands in David’s line (Ruth 2:12; 4:17); the book reports this as written and assigns it to the Prophecy Program.'])]),
(5, 'Israel, the Law, and the Covenants in Ruth', [('p', 'This section helps you see how Ruth speaks about Israel, the law, and the covenants without mixing programs. Ruth operates within Israel’s prophetic framework. By gathering these points together, this section shows you how to read Ruth in its own setting — as an Israel-program book — while recognizing what it says (and does not say) about the Body of Christ.'), ('p', 'Ruth reinforces Israel’s:'), ('ul', ['land and inheritance within the tribes (Ruth 4:10)', 'kinsman’s right to redeem (Ruth 3:9; 4:10)', 'the LORD God of Israel (Ruth 2:12)', 'the line to David (Ruth 4:17, 22)']), ('p', 'All content belongs exclusively to the Prophecy Program.')]),
(6, 'Israel-Program Distinctions', [('p', 'This section highlights the teachings in Ruth that belong uniquely to Israel’s prophetic program — truths rooted in prophecy, covenant promises, national identity, and God’s dealings with Israel. By gathering these together, this section helps you see how Ruth contributes to Israel’s story without borrowing from the Body-of-Christ program.'), ('ul', ['Ruth Joins Israel’s People — “thy people shall be my people, and thy God my God.” (Ruth 1:16).', 'Under the Wings of the LORD God of Israel — “under whose wings thou art come to trust.” (Ruth 2:12).', 'The Near Kinsman — “near of kin unto us, one of our next kinsmen.” (Ruth 2:20).', 'Asking the Kinsman — “for thou art a near kinsman.” (Ruth 3:9).', 'The Inheritance Purchased — “to raise up the name of the dead upon his inheritance.” (Ruth 4:10).', 'A Kinsman for Naomi — “Blessed be the LORD, which hath not left thee this day without a kinsman.” (Ruth 4:14).', 'The Line to David — “Obed begat Jesse, and Jesse begat David.” (Ruth 4:22).'])]),
(7, 'Truths Table: What Ruth Says', [('p', 'This table gathers the key statements, instructions, and teachings in Ruth and shows which ones belong to Israel’s prophetic program. Each row lists a truth from the book, explains what it says, and identifies its program identity based on Israel’s covenant setting. This helps you see, at a glance, what Ruth teaches directly to Israel and keeps those truths separate from universal principles that apply more broadly.'), ('table', ['Category', 'Truth Revealed in Ruth', 'Program Identity', 'Scripture'], [['Ruth Joins Israel’s People', '“thy people shall be my people, and thy God my God.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Ruth 1:16'], ['Under the Wings of the LORD God of Israel', '“under whose wings thou art come to trust.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Ruth 2:12'], ['The Near Kinsman', '“near of kin unto us, one of our next kinsmen.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Ruth 2:20'], ['Asking the Kinsman', '“for thou art a near kinsman.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Ruth 3:9'], ['The Inheritance Purchased', '“to raise up the name of the dead upon his inheritance.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Ruth 4:10'], ['A Kinsman for Naomi', '“Blessed be the LORD, which hath not left thee this day without a kinsman.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Ruth 4:14'], ['The Line to David', '“Obed begat Jesse, and Jesse begat David.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Ruth 4:22']], [20, 34, 26, 20])]),
(8, 'Universal Truths Table (All People, All Time)', [('p', 'This table gathers the truths in Ruth that apply to everyone, not just Israel. These are principles rooted in God’s character — things that hold true across all programs and all ages. By separating these universal truths from Israel’s covenant-specific instructions, this section helps you see what Ruth teaches all believers everywhere and what it teaches Israel in its own setting.'), ('table', ['Universal Truth', 'Description', 'Scripture'], [['Kindness Remembered', '“Blessed be he of the LORD, who hath not left off his kindness to the living and to the dead.”', 'Ruth 2:20'], ['A Virtuous Woman', '“all the city of my people doth know that thou art a virtuous woman.”', 'Ruth 3:11'], ['The LORD Gives Life', '“the LORD gave her conception, and she bare a son.”', 'Ruth 4:13']], [28, 48, 24])]),
(9, 'Program-Safe Application Grid', [('p', 'This section shows how to apply the teachings of Ruth without mixing programs. The applications come from Israel’s covenant story and from universal truths that apply to everyone. By keeping the applications tied to what the book actually says — and not borrowing commands from Israel’s covenants for the Body of Christ — this grid helps you respond to Ruth in a way that is faithful to its setting, clear, and safe.'), ('ol', ['<b>Show faithful kindness</b> — To the living and the dead (Ruth 2:20).', '<b>Pursue a good name</b> — A virtuous character is known (Ruth 3:11).', '<b>Thank the LORD for His gifts</b> — Life and children are from Him (Ruth 4:13).'])]),
(10, 'Teaching Outline for Ruth', [('p', 'This outline gives you a teacher-ready structure for presenting the book. It follows the narrative movements and highlights the major themes, making it easy to teach Ruth in classes, small groups, or personal study.'), ('olr', ['Naomi and Ruth (Ch. 1) — Loyalty and a vow. <i>(Ruth 1:16–17)</i>', 'Gleaning in Boaz’s Field (Ch. 2) — Blessing and kindness. <i>(Ruth 2:12, 20)</i>', 'At the Threshing Floor (Ch. 3) — The near kinsman. <i>(Ruth 3:9, 11)</i>', 'The Redemption (Ch. 4) — The line to David. <i>(Ruth 4:10, 13–14, 17, 22)</i>'])]),
(11, 'Companion Usage and Study Suggestions', [('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Personal Study'), ('p', 'This Companion is designed to walk alongside you as you read Ruth, helping you see both the prophetic structure of the book and the timeless truths rooted in God’s character. Use it slowly, chapter by chapter, letting the narrative movements, themes, and universal truths deepen your understanding. The Program-Safe Applications will guide your heart toward kindness, loyalty, and trust without confusing Israel’s covenant commands with the Body of Christ’s calling today.'), ('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Small Groups or Classes'), ('p', 'This Companion provides a clear, structured framework that makes teaching Ruth simple and accessible. Each section gives you ready-to-use material: narrative movements for overview, prophecy-program themes for doctrinal clarity, universal truths for discussion, and a pastoral teaching outline for weekly lessons. Use the tables to help your group distinguish Israel’s covenant identity from universal principles, and use the Program-Safe Application Grid to foster meaningful, Christ-centered conversation.'), ('h3', 'How This Companion Fits Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series'), ('p', 'This Companion is part of a larger series designed to help believers read every book of Scripture without blurring God’s prophetic program for Israel and His mystery program for the Body of Christ. Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series, Ruth stands firmly in Israel’s covenant story, yet it still reveals universal truths that shape Christian character today. This Companion shows you how to honor the book’s prophetic context while drawing out the timeless principles that apply to all believers in every age.')])]
